    "content": "Rick Pitino (born 1952) is a Hall of Fame basketball coach renowned for reviving college programs and becoming the first coach to win NCAA championships at two different schools (Kentucky 1996, Louisville 2013). His 50-year career includes head coaching roles at BU, Providence, Kentucky, Louisville, Iona, and St. John's, with NBA stints at the Knicks and Celtics. Major Coaching Stops Boston University (1978–1983): Began his head coaching career, turning around a struggling program. Providence College (1985–1987): Led the Friars to the 1987 Final Four. New York Knicks (1987–1989): Head coach in the NBA. University of Kentucky (1989–1997): Won the 1996 National Championship, reached three Final Fours, and dominated the SEC. Boston Celtics (1997–2001): Served as head coach and team president. University of Louisville (2001–2017): Won the 2013 National Championship (later vacated) and reached three Final Fours. Panathinaikos (2018–2020): Coached in Greece. Iona University (2020–2023): Led the Gaels to NCAA tournament appearances. St. John's University (2023–present): Currently leading the Red Storm. Key Achievements NCAA Champion: 1996 (Kentucky), 2013 (Louisville - vacated). Final Fours: 6 (1 Providence, 3 Kentucky, 2 Louisville). First Coach to lead three different schools to the Final Four. First Coach to win NCAA titles at two different schools. Career Record: Over 890 wins (as of March 2026). Controversies His tenure at Louisville ended following a 2017 FBI investigation into college basketball corruption, leading to the vacating of 123 wins, including the 2012-2015 seasons.",
